@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix pmp:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/practices/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

#
# prima-materia — the waiver that runs both ways, examined, and the statement
# it extends
#
# pmc:TheWaiverRunsBothWays stood at the head of
# queries/untested-generalizations.rq with four witnesses. Its own note named
# the falsifier that would break it and the earlier statement with it: a
# transmission that waives a requirement on the body or on conduct, for either
# class, at either end. This protocol reads every release the holding records
# against the statement, opens one work outside its three transmissions, and
# finds the falsifier three times in that work's base text. The second protocol
# in this file records the same reading against
# pmc:AttainmentRelievesOnlyTheOutwardCondition, whose whole content the
# falsifier contradicts.
#
# The searches carried for it are in
# examinations/searches-waiver-runs-both-ways.ttl.
#

pmp:WaiverRunsBothWaysExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Whether outward requirements are released at both ends and requirements on body and conduct at neither"@en, "Ob äußere Forderungen an beiden Enden erlassen werden und Forderungen an Körper und Verhalten an keinem"@de ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that where an operative transmission releases anyone from a requirement fixed outside the practitioner it releases at both ends, the attained and the one who cannot meet it, the second with a second route; that it releases nobody from a requirement on body or conduct; and that the second kind is the one it names when it accounts for a user in whom nothing happened. The population is every node of the holding on main on 2026-10-22 that records a transmission releasing somebody from, or giving a second route past, a requirement it sets for its own procedure: drawn by a scan of every literal of the compiled graph for waive, exempt, excused, dispense, need not, not necessary, unnecessary, released from, substitute, concession, relax, cannot afford, who cannot, unable to, not required and no longer needs, which returned 161 nodes, every one read at the node. One work was opened, the Haṭhayogapradīpikā with Brahmānanda's commentary, because it sets a built place, hours, a material, a diet and a continence in one regimen."@en ;
    pm:tests pmc:TheWaiverRunsBothWays ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "As the statement names it: an operative transmission that sets an outward requirement, releases its adepts from it, and gives the practitioner who cannot meet it no second route but exclusion; or one that waives a bodily or conduct requirement — a fast, a washing, a term of continence, a rule of diet or of anger — for either class, at either end. The second is met three times in one work, in its base text and each time for the practitioner who has attained: the Haṭhayogapradīpikā lifts its milk-and-ghee diet once the practice is firm (II.14), limits the avoidance of fire, women and the road to the beginning (I.61, which its commentary spells out as allowing the accomplished householder his own wife in her season), and sets aside the rules of yoga, continence first in the commentary's gloss, for the one who knows vajrolī (III.83). The first is not met anywhere in the holding."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:casesWithDeviation ;
    pm:baseRate "Of the 161 nodes, twenty-five record a release from or a second route past a requirement the same transmission sets or bear on one as a near case, and the opened work added five releases in six nodes. Requirements fixed outside the practitioner: twenty releases in twelve transmissions — three upward, for attainment (the Key of Solomon on days and hours, the Baopuzi on the calendar, the Hasidic testament on seclusion in a house full of people), fourteen downward or for a circumstance, every one with a second route (Caraka's hut and calendar and his substitute drugs, the Heptameron's Moon and vestment, Abramelin's oratory for the sick, the Ghāya's mended Moon for work that cannot wait, the Rasaśāstra's wife replaced by a course of sulphur, the demotic papyrus's boy replaced by an ointment, the Mandaean consecration by one priest in necessity, the Tungus costume and the substitute mirror, the Ignatian week lengthened for the slow, the Hasidic companion in one room, and in the opened work the three junctures for the one unable at midnight and the buffalo's milk), and three tied to a part of the apparatus or a material and not to a practitioner (the Liber iuratus's ten orations, the Rasaśāstra's cinnabar mercury twice). Requirements on body and conduct: three releases in the opened work, all upward and all in the base text; three near cases in the holding (the Nāth purity, the Lakota shaman's release, the Shangqing pledge in place of the hair); two releases of a requirement on the person treated and not on the practitioner (the Pulling Book, Suśruta); two that release no requirement the transmission sets (the Theravāda handbook's exemption of those of great past endeavour from the whole course, which is a training and names only the dead, and Zhu Xi's leave to the weary to sit without a monk's posture, which his transmission never required); and no release anywhere for the practitioner who cannot meet a requirement on body or conduct."@en ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchFoundOpposition ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Searched: the 161 nodes of the compiled holding of 2026-10-22 returned by the scan in the definition, each read at the node; the Haṭhayogapradīpikā in the Sanskrit of the Bombay printing with the Jyotsnā, I.11-17, I.56-64, II.10-14 and III.83-85 read continuously and the whole text layer searched for the terms listed at pmp:WaiverHathapradipikaSearch and pmp:WaiverHathapradipikaMathaSearch, with Sinh's and Iyangar's translations at the same verses. Found against the statement: the three releases of the opened work from requirements on conduct and diet. Found for it: the opened work's two downward releases of outward requirements, each with a second route, and its hermitage, which nobody is let off. Not searched: works the holding has not opened, the parts of the opened work not read, and releases the scan's vocabulary does not reach — a release worded as an allowance, a permission or a time limit, like the one at I.61, is reached only by reading, and the four stretches read are twenty-four verses of nearly four hundred."@en ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-22" ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-10-22" ;
    skos:note "The second clause falls, and it falls in the shape its own note feared least. The note expected a transmission to let its adepts off a fast or a washing, and the Haṭhayogapradīpikā does exactly that, three times and in its own verses: a dietary rule for the first period, lifted when the practice is firm, which the commentary dates to the accomplishment of the retention alone (pmc:HathaKsirajyaNiyamaDrdhabhyase); the avoidance of fire, women and the road set for the beginning only (pmc:HathaAdauVarjana), which the commentary turns into an explicit allowance for the accomplished (pmc:JyotsnaSiddheAbhyaseKadacit); and the rules of yoga as a whole set aside for the one who knows vajrolī by his own experience (pmc:HathaVajroliNiyamairVina). The continence that the first chapter makes a condition of its term of a year (pmc:HathaSiddhiPresupposesBrahmacarya) is the requirement the work most plainly releases."@en ;
    skos:note "The same work runs the outward half of the statement the other way round. Its hermitage is released for nobody (pmp:WaiverHathapradipikaMathaSearch); its hour and its material are released only for the one who cannot meet them, with a second route, and only in the commentary (pmc:JyotsnaTrisandhyamAsaktasya, pmc:JyotsnaGavyalabheMahisa). In this transmission attainment buys relief from the body's rules and from nothing outside the body, which is the inverse of the pattern the statement's three transmissions showed. The statement's sorting of requirements into two classes survives the reading; what it said about which class each end of the scale may let go does not."@en ;
    skos:note "The first clause is not falsified, and it is weaker than its wording. Its named falsifier — adepts released, the unable excluded — is met nowhere: every downward release in the holding gives a second route, and the one exclusion the holding records, the Tungus shaman who is old and weak, turns on his body and not on an outward requirement. But the clause says that a transmission which releases an outward requirement releases it at both ends, and read transmission by transmission that holds only in the Solomonic corpus, where the Key releases upward and the Heptameron and Abramelin downward, and in a looser form in the Hasidic testament. Nine transmissions release in one direction only, eight of them downward, and the Liber iuratus releases a part of its apparatus for every practitioner alike. The both-ways shape of the statement was formed by pooling ends that belong to different transmissions: the Caraka gave the lower end and the Key and the Baopuzi the upper."@en ;
    skos:note "The third clause, that the requirement on body and conduct is the one named when nothing happened, holds in the opened work and is blurred in one other. The Haṭhayogapradīpikā names six things by which yoga is destroyed and all six are dispositions or acts of the practitioner (pmc:HathaSadbhirYogoVinasyati). The Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm releases its Moon for urgent work by a second configuration, and then names celestial configurations — the twenty-fourth mansion, a crooked ascendant — as causes of a spoiled work, beside the maker's resolve; it names both kinds and not one (pmc:GhayaKusufAlQamar and the counts at pmt:IslamicLetterMagic)."@en ;
    skos:note "The near cases, with what keeps each from settling the second clause on its own. The Nāth verse that whoever recites oṃ, pure or impure, is not smeared by sin releases a condition of purity for everybody at the point of practice, and Mahīdhara's Hindi commentary narrows it to the one who has exchanged outer purity for inner (pmc:NathPranavaJapaAlepa); whether the Gorakṣaśataka itself sets the purity it releases was not established. The Lakota candidate's rules of conduct in the sacred lodge can be released by a shaman (pmc:CautionOnTheCandidatesConduct), a release by a third party for no stated class. The Shangqing closing chapter replaces the cutting of the hair by a pledge of brocade, so that skin and flesh stay whole (pmc:ShangqingHuangtingMengjin), a bodily act of the rite of transmission replaced for everybody, which is a reform of the rite more than a release of a practitioner."@en ;
    skos:note "What the reading supports instead, recorded here and not written as a node, because SPEC §16 requires the counter-search against the holding before a generalization is written and this one was formed from the same reading that would have to search it. Across the holding, a requirement fixed outside the practitioner is released far more often for the one who cannot meet it, with a second route, than for the one who has attained; a requirement on the practitioner's body or conduct is released in no transmission for the one who cannot meet it, and where it is released at all, in the Haṭhayogapradīpikā and at a later station of the Nāth corpus, it is released for the one who has attained. The asymmetry the statement put between the two classes of requirement survives at the lower end and is gone at the upper."@en .

pmp:AttainmentRelievesOutwardExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Whether what attainment is allowed to waive is only the condition fixed outside the practitioner"@en, "Ob das, was die Erlangung erlassen darf, nur die außerhalb des Übenden festgesetzte Bedingung ist"@de ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that where an operative transmission relieves a practitioner of one of its own prescribed conditions on the ground of what he has attained, the condition relieved is fixed outside him and does not bear on his body or conduct, and that where the same transmissions speak of a bodily condition and attainment together they say attainment does not relieve it. The population and the reading are those of pmp:WaiverRunsBothWaysExamination in this file: the 161 nodes of the holding of 2026-10-22, and the Haṭhayogapradīpikā with Brahmānanda's commentary at I.11-17, I.56-64, II.10-14 and III.83-85. The statement is the narrower predecessor of pmc:TheWaiverRunsBothWays, and that statement's note says the falsifier of its second clause would break this one with it."@en ;
    pm:tests pmc:AttainmentRelievesOnlyTheOutwardCondition ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "As the statement names it: a passage in any operative transmission granting an experienced practitioner relief from a bodily or conduct condition — a fast, a term of continence, a washing, a dietary rule — on the ground of his attainment, stated as a waiver of a rule the same text imposes on others. Met three times in the base text of the Haṭhayogapradīpikā: the dietary rule of milk and ghee lifted once the practice is firm (II.14), the avoidance of women, fire and the road set for the beginning (I.61), the rules of yoga, continence first, set aside for the one who knows vajrolī (III.83). Met once more at a later station of the Nāth corpus, where Mahīdhara's commentary confines the verse on reciting oṃ pure or impure to the one who has given up outer purity for inner."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:casesWithDeviation ;
    pm:baseRate "Of the releases the holding and the opened work record, six are granted on the ground of attainment. Three release a requirement fixed outside the practitioner — the Key of Solomon's days and hours, the Baopuzi's calendar, the Hasidic testament's seclusion — and three a requirement on conduct and diet, all three in one work, the Haṭhayogapradīpikā. The Nāth commentary is a seventh at a later station. The holding records two refusals of an attainment release for a bodily condition, the Zhen'gao on emission and the Shangqing term of eighteen years, both in one transmission."@en ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchFoundOpposition ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Searched as in pmp:WaiverRunsBothWaysExamination. Found against the statement: the three places of the Haṭhayogapradīpikā and the Nāth commentary. Found for it: the Key and the Baopuzi, its own witnesses, and the Hasidic testament as a third transmission releasing an outward requirement for attainment. The distinction the statement's own counter-search drew, between relieving a practitioner of a condition and setting different conditions for different classes from the outset, was put to the three places and does not save it: II.14 and I.61 set a rule for the first period of practice and lift it when the practice is firm, which is the shape the Key's own exemption has — extremely necessary for beginners, not necessary for adepts — and III.83 sets aside rules the work imposes on every other practitioner. Not searched: the works and stretches listed there."@en ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-22" ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-10-22" ;
    skos:note "The statement falls in both its sentences. Its first sentence said that what attainment relieves is fixed outside the practitioner; in the Haṭhayogapradīpikā what attainment relieves is a diet, a continence and the rules of yoga, and nothing outside the body. Its second said that where the same transmissions speak of a bodily condition and attainment together, attainment does not relieve it; the Haṭhayogapradīpikā speaks of exactly that pair three times and each time the attainment relieves it. The counter-case was in a work the holding had already opened, at verses nobody had entered, which is the situation SPEC §16 was written against: the counter-search before the statement was run against the nodes and not against the works they came from."@en ;
    skos:note "What survives is a fact about two transmissions and not a regularity: the Key and the Baopuzi relieve their adepts of the calendar and nothing else, and the Shangqing corpus refuses its advanced practitioners any relief from the bodily condition it states. A successor statement would have to say why the Haṭhayogapradīpikā sorts the other way, and the reading offers a reason it does not prove: the haṭha regimen treats diet, continence and avoidance as means to a bodily state, the firm practice or the retention alone, after which they have done their work, while the Key lets its adepts off the hours because their faith no longer needs them and keeps its bath, its fast and its confession for everybody. Whether a requirement is released on attainment may follow from whether the transmission treats it as a means or as a condition, and not from whether it lies inside or outside the body."@en .
